## What these files are

`gold116/` and `gold113/` contain **derived** ink-probability labels: single
`uint8` arrays per segment, resampled onto the coarse (116 keV / 113 keV)
surface-volume grid from ink predictions published by the Vesuvius Challenge on
the corresponding fine-resolution scans. They contain **no scroll CT data** and
no reconstruction of any scroll volume.

They are a transformation of two upstream sources:

1. **EduceLab-Scrolls** — the X-ray micro-CT scan data of the Herculaneum
   papyri, from which the surface volumes and meshes derive.
2. **Vesuvius Challenge open data** — the published ink-detection model outputs
   (model checkpoint `20260417190342`, recipe `new_canon_autoresearch_recipe`)
   and the `tifxyz` meshes used to place them on the coarse grid.

Because these are derivative works, **the terms of both upstream sources apply
in addition to this licence**, and where they are more restrictive, they
govern. In particular, redistribution and use of Vesuvius Challenge data are
subject to the **Vesuvius Challenge data agreement and contest terms** that
each user accepts when obtaining access to the open data
(https://scrollprize.org/data — see also https://scrollprize.org/terms). If you
have not accepted those terms, you are not licensed to use the upstream data,
and this licence cannot grant you that right.

